WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.689
Show proposed processes connecting climate, trees, production and activity.

00:00:06.145 --> 00:00:11.072
Consider management, land use, institutions and other pathways.

00:00:11.526 --> 00:00:15.941
Arrows indicate hypotheses, not proven causal effects.

00:00:16.395 --> 00:00:19.467
A diagram helps expose missing steps.

00:00:19.921 --> 00:00:26.296
Production, exchange or institutions may mediate a relationship between climate and society.

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:31.711
For each proposed arrow, ask what observation could support or weaken it.

00:00:32.171 --> 00:00:37.560
The diagram organizes a hypothesis; drawing it does not verify it.

00:00:38.363 --> 00:00:41.640
Specify place, period and material.

00:00:42.102 --> 00:00:46.227
State competing hypotheses and predicted observations.

00:00:46.681 --> 00:00:50.063
Identify evidence that could change the conclusion.

00:00:50.523 --> 00:00:55.221
A broad question such as whether climate affected history needs to become a specific

00:00:55.234 --> 00:00:56.497
comparison.

00:00:56.957 --> 00:01:00.786
Define a place, period, phenomenon and source.

00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:06.155
Include the observations expected under alternative explanations and decide how

00:01:06.168 --> 00:01:10.252
contradictory evidence would lead you to revise the claim.

00:01:11.055 --> 00:01:15.378
Check source location, preservation and access conditions.

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:20.307
Plan trees, sites, comparisons and sampling positions.

00:01:20.768 --> 00:01:24.788
Prepare alternatives if the preferred material is unavailable.

00:01:25.241 --> 00:01:29.722
A promising question still depends on suitable surviving material.

00:01:30.176 --> 00:01:35.320
Public images or existing measurements may offer alternatives to new sampling.

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:41.439
Check access and preservation needs for the actual objects involved, and include a workable

00:01:41.452 --> 00:01:45.632
alternative if the preferred material cannot be examined.

00:01:46.435 --> 00:01:50.278
Calendar dating needs crossdating and an anchored reference.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:56.510
Disturbance studies combine growth, injuries, populations and comparisons.

00:01:56.964 --> 00:02:01.950
Climate estimation needs instrumental calibration and independent testing.

00:02:02.411 --> 00:02:05.806
A list of method names is not yet a research plan.

00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:11.707
Explain which part of the question each method addresses and which other evidence it needs.

00:02:12.161 --> 00:02:17.345
Fine measurement resolution does not automatically provide equally fine resolution for an

00:02:17.358 --> 00:02:20.532
event or historical interpretation.

00:02:21.335 --> 00:02:26.106
Move from the abstract to methods, materials, figures and limits.

00:02:26.561 --> 00:02:31.094
Record dataset version, identifiers and the subset used.

00:02:31.548 --> 00:02:36.285
Distinguish primary reports from introductory or secondary accounts.

00:02:36.745 --> 00:02:41.706
An introductory article is useful for discovering a study, while the original report

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:44.350
provides its methods and conditions.

00:02:44.811 --> 00:02:50.423
Record the particular database entry and subset used, not just the portal address.

00:02:50.877 --> 00:02:56.166
Use the bilingual glossary to expand search terms and find primary material in both

00:02:56.179 --> 00:02:57.880
languages.

00:02:58.683 --> 00:03:02.835
Make plots with clear axes, units and sample depth.

00:03:03.291 --> 00:03:07.422
Distinguish measurements, synthetic data and schematics.

00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:11.975
Check the applicable conditions and credits for external figures.

00:03:12.435 --> 00:03:16.830
Redrawing a graph does not remove the need to credit its source data.

00:03:17.291 --> 00:03:22.883
Make it clear which parts are observations and which are settings chosen to explain an idea.

00:03:23.343 --> 00:03:28.606
For an external photograph or figure, check the conditions applying to that particular item

00:03:28.619 --> 00:03:31.436
and retain the required credits.

00:03:32.239 --> 00:03:38.404
Write the question, sources, methods, comparisons, limits and additional evidence.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:43.781
Choose one or more archaeological, ecological or climate perspectives.

00:03:44.241 --> 00:03:48.412
8 minutes: draft 5, discuss 2, revise 1.

00:03:48.873 --> 00:03:52.445
Pause and choose a topic related to your own interests.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:57.998
You can begin before locating every source if you specify what evidence is needed.

00:03:58.452 --> 00:04:01.524
Avoid writing the desired conclusion first.

00:04:01.978 --> 00:04:06.189
Describe the results that would support the proposed explanation and those that would

00:04:06.202 --> 00:04:08.520
require revising it.
